  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nDry weather with warmer than average temperatures through \nTuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure will remain to the east of the region. Due to the\ncenter of the high far off to the east, strong enough subsidence\nwill keep the region dry with the exception of a few showers \nand storms along the Quebec border this and tomorrow afternoon. \nIn addition, winds will remain fairly weak. Temperatures will \nremain warmer than average with upper 70s to mid 80s Tuesday. \nPatchy valley fog again tonight, with perhaps a bit more fog \nespecially near the coast Tuesday night as southerly marine flow\nbegins to increase ahead of the next system.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A system is likely to move in later in the \nweek, bringing another round of wetting rainfall into the \nweekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure system stalls over the Northeast, eventually moving\neast late week. Low will hopefully be over the Gulf of Maine by\nWednesday night into Friday. Wetting rainfall expected, with \nhigher totals along Downeast, as early as Wednesday night or\nThursday. Granted, this system has been on a constant delay,\nsince models are struggling to resolve when the system will\neventually move east. Potential for 0.5 to 1 inch of rainfall. \nThere is some potential for a thunderstorm along the frontal\nboundary, but instability is minimal.",
